= Collective intelligence — Technical =

'''Collective intelligence''' is task-level cognitive performance generated by aggregation or interaction among multiple contributors. A technical treatment should distinguish the statistical benefits of pooling independent judgments from genuinely interactive processes such as communication, specialization, coordination, and joint representation.

The appropriate object of measurement is not a metaphysical collective mind but a mapping

<math>F:(x_1,\ldots,x_n,\Gamma,T)\rightarrow y,</math>

where <math>x_i</math> represents participant capabilities or information, <math>\Gamma</math> the interaction structure, <math>T</math> the task, and <math>y</math> the collective output.


Collective intelligence should be distinguished from [[Group cognition]], which concerns cognitive processes attributable to interacting groups, and from [[Swarm intelligence]], which emphasizes decentralized coordination by relatively simple agents. It can also depend on [[Social information processing]] and [[Distributed cognition]] when information and task structure are spread across people and artifacts.

== Independent aggregation ==

Suppose <math>n</math> participants estimate quantity <math>\theta</math>:

<math>X_i=\theta+\epsilon_i.</math>

If errors are unbiased with common variance <math>\sigma^2</math> and pairwise correlation <math>\rho</math>, the variance of the average is

<math>\operatorname{Var}(\bar X)=\frac{\sigma^2}{n}[1+(n-1)\rho].</math>

When <math>\rho=0</math>,

<math>\operatorname{Var}(\bar X)=\sigma^2/n.</math>

When <math>\rho\rightarrow1</math>, adding participants provides little reduction in error.

This gives a precise reason why crowd size alone is insufficient: epistemic independence matters.

== Bias ==

If each estimate has common bias <math>b</math>,

<math>E[\bar X]=\theta+b.</math>

Averaging reduces random error but not shared bias.

Many-person agreement can therefore remain systematically wrong.

Correlated data sources, common training, shared institutions, and algorithmic recommendation can all create common bias.

== Weighted aggregation ==

When estimators have unequal variances, a weighted aggregate

<math>\hat\theta=\sum_i w_iX_i,\qquad \sum_i w_i=1</math>

can improve performance.

Under independent unbiased Gaussian errors, inverse-variance weights

<math>w_i\propto 1/\sigma_i^2</math>

minimize variance.

In practice, reliability estimates are uncertain and performance can change over time.

Reputation weighting should therefore be calibrated on observed outcomes rather than status.

== Median and robust aggregation ==

Means are sensitive to extreme values.

For heavy-tailed estimates or adversarial contamination, median, trimmed means, or other robust estimators can outperform arithmetic averaging.

The correct aggregation rule depends on the distribution and loss function.

There is no universal “crowd average.”

== Condorcet jury theorem ==

Suppose <math>n</math> voters independently choose between two alternatives and each selects the correct alternative with probability <math>p>1/2</math>.

For odd <math>n</math>, majority accuracy is

<math>P_{maj}=\sum_{k=(n+1)/2}^{n}{n\choose k}p^k(1-p)^{n-k}.</math>

Under independence and identical competence, <math>P_{maj}\rightarrow1</math> as <math>n\rightarrow\infty</math>.

The theorem is powerful but conditional.

If <math>p<1/2</math>, increasing group size makes majority error more likely. Correlated voters also invalidate the simple independence result.

== Diversity prediction ==

A useful general principle is that aggregate performance depends on both individual quality and diversity of errors.

For squared-error prediction, identities related to the diversity-prediction framework show that ensemble error can be reduced when individual errors differ.

This does not imply social or demographic diversity automatically improves every task. The relevant diversity must affect the information or solution space.

== Social influence ==

Let initial estimates be vector <math>\mathbf x(0)</math>. A DeGroot-style social-learning model updates

<math>\mathbf x(t+1)=W\mathbf x(t),</math>

where <math>W</math> is a row-stochastic influence matrix.

Under common conditions, estimates converge.

Consensus is not necessarily accuracy.

If influential nodes are biased or information diversity disappears, convergence can move the group away from truth.

Lorenz et al. experimentally found that exposure to others' estimates narrowed diversity and undermined a wisdom-of-crowds effect in their estimation tasks.<ref name="lorenz">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21576485/ Lorenz, J. et al. (2011), “How social influence can undermine the wisdom of crowd effect,” ''PNAS'' 108(22), 9020–9025.]</ref>

== Communication topology ==

Let group communication be graph

<math>G=(V,E).</math>

Topology affects:

* information diffusion;
* bottlenecks;
* redundancy;
* subgroup isolation;
* centralization;
* resilience.

A complete graph maximizes direct communication but can create high attention cost.

Sparse modular networks can preserve diverse exploration while limiting information flow.

Optimal topology depends on task interdependence and communication cost.

== Task decomposition ==

Let task <math>T</math> decompose into subtasks

<math>T=\{t_1,\ldots,t_m\}.</math>

Assign participant <math>i</math> to subtask <math>j</math> with expected performance <math>s_{ij}</math>.

A simplified allocation problem is

<math>\max_X\sum_{i,j}X_{ij}s_{ij}</math>

subject to capacity and dependency constraints.

However, subtasks can be coupled.

Integration cost <math>C_I</math> may increase with the number of interfaces among components.

Large teams can therefore lose efficiency when coordination exceeds gains from specialization.

== Common group-performance factor ==

Woolley et al. measured groups across several tasks and used factor analysis to identify a common group-performance factor.<ref name="woolley">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20929725/ Woolley, A. W. et al. (2010), “Evidence for a collective intelligence factor in the performance of human groups,” ''Science'' 330(6004), 686–688.]</ref>

Riedl et al. later analyzed 22 studies with 5,279 individuals in 1,356 groups and reported robust evidence for a CI factor and out-of-sample prediction across tasks.<ref name="riedl">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8166150/ Riedl, C. et al. (2021), “Quantifying collective intelligence in human groups,” ''PNAS'' 118(21), e2005737118.]</ref>

Factor scores depend on task sampling and measurement model.

A general CI factor should therefore be interpreted as a statistical latent variable within the studied task domain, not as a directly observed substance.

== Group composition ==

Group composition variables can include:

* member cognitive skill;
* expertise diversity;
* social perceptiveness;
* demographic composition;
* prior experience;
* motivation.

Woolley et al. reported associations with social sensitivity and conversational turn-taking. Their observed association with proportion of women was mediated in later analyses by social perceptiveness rather than supporting a biological sex interpretation.

Composition effects should be analyzed through plausible mechanisms and replication.

== Group process ==

Riedl et al. reported that collaboration process predicted collective intelligence strongly relative to composition variables in their datasets.<ref name="riedl" />

Process variables can include:

* speaking-time distribution;
* response to dissent;
* information sharing;
* leadership;
* turn-taking;
* task assignment;
* conflict resolution.

Causal interpretation requires experimental manipulation or strong identification, not correlation alone.

== Hidden-profile problems ==

In hidden-profile tasks, information needed for the best solution is distributed among members, while some information is shared.

Groups can fail because shared information is discussed more than unique information.

This is a canonical example of dispersed knowledge not automatically becoming collective intelligence.

Interventions can explicitly solicit unique evidence before deliberation.

== Brainstorming and production blocking ==

Interactive brainstorming can suffer from production blocking because only one participant speaks at a time, evaluation apprehension, or social loafing.

Nominal groups that generate ideas independently before aggregation can sometimes outperform fully interactive brainstorming.

The lesson generalizes: interaction is not automatically beneficial.

== Forecasting ensembles ==

For probabilistic forecasts <math>p_i(y)</math>, an ensemble can use

<math>p_{ens}(y)=\sum_i w_ip_i(y).</math>

Weights can be uniform or learned from historical performance.

Evaluate forecasts with proper scoring rules such as log loss or Brier score.

Calibration and sharpness should be reported separately.

== Prediction markets ==

Prediction markets aggregate private beliefs through trading.

Under appropriate incentives and liquidity, prices can encode useful forecasts.

Market price is still influenced by wealth, risk preferences, transaction costs, and strategic behavior.

Prediction markets are one collective-intelligence mechanism, not proof that all markets are intelligent.

== Crowdsourcing ==

A crowdsourcing system can be modeled as:

<pre>
task decomposition
→ participant recruitment
→ contribution
→ quality control
→ aggregation
→ feedback
</pre>

Quality control may use redundancy, gold-standard tasks, peer review, reputation, or statistical inference.

Redundant labeling improves reliability only if errors are sufficiently independent and annotators understand the task.

== Collective search ==

For complex search spaces, groups can balance exploration and exploitation.

Let each agent search region <math>S_i</math> and share candidate solutions.

Too much communication can cause premature convergence; too little can duplicate work.

Adaptive systems can delay sharing or preserve parallel subgroups to maintain exploration.

== Multi-agent systems ==

In a [[Multi-agent system]], collective performance can use:

* consensus protocols;
* auctions;
* voting;
* blackboard architectures;
* shared memory;
* emergent coordination;
* reinforcement learning.

Evaluate system-level performance relative to:

* best single agent;
* average agent;
* noncommunicating ensemble;
* centralized planner;
* randomized baseline.

Without those comparisons, “collective intelligence” can become a label rather than a demonstrated property.

== Human–AI teams ==

For human–AI system <math>H+A</math>, a synergy claim requires

<math>P(H+A)>\max(P(H),P(A))</math>

under comparable resources for a relevant performance measure <math>P</math>.

Even this inequality may not establish general synergy if the system uses more time or compute.

Resource-normalized evaluation should include:

* accuracy;
* latency;
* cost;
* robustness;
* calibration;
* provenance.

== Correlated AI errors ==

Suppose AI-assisted participants share model error <math>\epsilon_M</math>:

<math>\epsilon_i=\epsilon_M+\eta_i.</math>

Then pairwise correlation can remain high even when participants act independently after receiving the model output.

This can collapse wisdom-of-crowds benefits.

Independent models are not necessarily independent either if they share training data or architectures.

== Adversarial and strategic agents ==

Collectives can include participants with conflicting goals.

Robust aggregation must tolerate:

* malicious reports;
* sybil identities;
* collusion;
* strategic withholding.

Byzantine-resilient consensus, reputation, cryptographic identity, and mechanism design address different aspects.

Epistemic reliability and strategic robustness should be evaluated separately.

== Collective intelligence versus collective consciousness ==

A group-level performance variable does not entail phenomenal consciousness.

The inference

<pre>
coordinated performance
→ intelligence metric
→ conscious group mind
</pre>

is invalid without additional theory and evidence.

The same boundary applies to animal swarms and artificial multi-agent systems.

== Evaluation protocol ==

A strong CI study should define:

# task distribution;
# individual baseline;
# aggregation baseline;
# interaction protocol;
# resource budget;
# performance metric;
# independence structure;
# uncertainty;
# held-out tasks.

Where generalization is claimed, test on tasks not used to construct the collective score.

== Failure modes ==

Failure modes include:

* common bias;
* herding;
* information cascades;
* domination;
* communication bottlenecks;
* hidden expertise;
* correlated training data;
* social loafing;
* poor decomposition;
* metric gaming.

